# =============================================================================
# site.conf.example -- optional site configuration
#
# Copy to site.conf next to the scripts (or point $SITE_CONF at it) to override
# the defaults in the scripts' configuration blocks. This keeps one script set
# for the whole landscape with all site-specific values in a single file.
#
#   cp site.conf.example site.conf
#   $EDITOR site.conf
#
# Search order in every script:
#   1. $SITE_CONF, if set and readable
#   2. site.conf in the script's own directory (symlinks resolved)
#   3. site.conf in each directory of $SITE_CONF_DIRS
#
# So a conf placed next to the scripts is picked up automatically. For a script
# that lives elsewhere -- the cockpit script belongs in the <sid>adm home --
# either export SITE_CONF_DIRS, or name the file explicitly in cron:
#   SITE_CONF=/opt/sap-acme/site.conf "$HOME"/sap_hana_cockpit_cert_renew.sh verify
#   SITE_CONF_DIRS=/opt/sap-acme      "$HOME"/sap_hana_cockpit_cert_renew.sh verify
#
# Which file was loaded is recorded in the context line of renew.log
# ("conf=..."), so a conf that is silently not found is visible there.
#
# CAUTION: the file is sourced as shell code and runs with the calling script's
# privileges (<sid>adm or root). Give it the same protection as the scripts:
# owner root, mode 644, never group- or world-writable. Do not put passwords
# here -- those belong in the hdbuserstore and in ~/.xsa_cert_renew.pw.
#
# Every variable is optional; omit what you do not need. Variables a given
# script does not know are simply ignored by it.
#
# Note: site.conf is deliberately in .gitignore -- keep your real values out of
# the repository.#
